# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in the values from your Supabase
# project. .env.local is gitignored; this template is not, so never put a
# real value in here.
#
#   cp client/.env.local.example client/.env.local
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which key goes here
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Supabase now issues two keys, and only one of them belongs in this file:
#
#   sb_publishable_...   THIS ONE. Browser-safe, obeys row-level security.
#                        Settings → API Keys → "Publishable key".
#                        It replaces what used to be called the "anon" key,
#                        which is why some docs and older projects still use
#                        that name for the same thing.
#
#   sb_secret_...        NOT THIS ONE. It bypasses row-level security
#                        completely, so it is full admin access to the
#                        database. Server-side only: never in this directory,
#                        never with a VITE_ prefix, never in the bundle.
#
# The variable name below is just a label we chose; the client reads either
# VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY or the older VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY. Only the
# value matters, and the value is the publishable key.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why the old names disappeared
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The legacy anon/service_role pair were JWTs signed with the project's JWT
# secret. Rotating a leaked one meant rotating that secret, which invalidated
# every logged-in session. The new keys are opaque and revocable on their own,
# so the dashboard lets you mint several and retire them one at a time.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These values are public, and that is fine
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Vite inlines anything prefixed VITE_ into the browser bundle, so both values
# below are visible to every visitor. That is by design: the publishable key
# grants exactly what the row-level security policies in
# supabase/migrations/0001_init.sql allow. Those policies are the security
# boundary, not the key — which is why RLS is enabled in the same migration
# that creates the tables rather than as a follow-up.

VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_...
